1
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:11,940
Greetings and welcome to the lecture on the biosafety officer. In this lecture I will

2
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:17,860
introduce you to the roles and responsibilities of a biosafety officer. The biosafety officer

3
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:24,580
represents the human component of the biosafety system of an organization. As a biosafety officer

4
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:30,660
you are expected to be cognizant of uphold and implement the principles policies of the

5
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:37,140
organization within the framework of biarisk management. You should be aware of the key

6
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:42,180
concepts associated with biological systems and should be cognizant of biosafety guidelines,

7
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:49,300
laws and practices. Now in this particular module I will delve into the roles and responsibilities

8
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:56,180
of a biosafety officer and also introduce you to what is known as the culture of biosafety within

9
00:00:56,180 --> 00:01:07,420
an organization. The objectives of this module are to introduce you to the culture of biosafety,

10
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:13,100
firstly to introduce you to what is termed as compliance and to give you an overview of the

11
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:20,660
roles and responsibilities of a biosafety officer or a biarisk manager. Upon completion of this

12
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:25,620
module you should be able to describe the roles and responsibilities of the biosafety officer,

13
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:29,860
provide an overview of the guidelines which are the driving principles of biosafety,

14
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,980
develop the first component of your biosafety manual which is the roles and responsibilities

15
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:40,740
of a biosafety officer and apply what you have learned to inculcate a culture of biosafety at

16
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:52,420
your respective institutions. Now as an exercise I want you to develop a guideline on the roles and

17
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:58,740
responsibilities of a biosafety officer. Do this from the perspective of establishing your

18
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:04,660
career as a biosafety officer or as a biarisk manager. Define your roles and responsibilities

19
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:13,140
in your book and I will delve into these roles and responsibilities as we progress with this

20
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:23,740
lecture. The first concept which I want to introduce you is to the word culture of biosafety

21
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:32,510
and I have taken this paragraph from Texas Agricultural and Mechanical University

22
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:38,510
which defines culture and what exactly is culture. Culture is a way of life of a group of people,

23
00:02:39,230 --> 00:02:46,430
the behavior, beliefs, values and symbols that they accept generally without thinking about them

24
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:52,270
and that are passed along by communication and imitation from one generation to the next.

25
00:02:53,950 --> 00:03:00,190
Now what you need to inculcate in your organization is the culture of biosafety

26
00:03:00,270 --> 00:03:08,670
and this involves multiple steps which focus on acculturation and this leads to a change in

27
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:16,670
the behavior, a change in the system of beliefs, values and symbols and these changes have to be

28
00:03:16,670 --> 00:03:23,630
done subconsciously or without thinking and these changes are passed on from one generation

29
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:30,830
of biosafety officers to the other or along the hierarchy via communication and via imitation.

30
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:40,030
This is why it's always good to practice all what you preach so that your peers and your junior

31
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:51,260
officers learn from you by imitation. So culture is basically defined by the Texas A&M

32
00:03:51,420 --> 00:04:00,940
paragraph is based on behavior, beliefs, values and symbols all of which are elements of biosafety

33
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:11,460
management. I would also like to quote from Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi who says if we could change

34
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:17,540
ourselves the tendencies in the world would also change as a man or woman changes his or her own

35
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:24,660
nature so does the attitude of the world change towards him, her and we need not wait to see what

36
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:30,580
others do. What this essentially means is that change begins with you so as a biosafety officer

37
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:38,770
you become the role model for the institution. The next factor which I want to introduce you

38
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:44,610
is what is known as the culture of compliance. The culture of compliance means confirming to a

39
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:52,050
rule such as a specification policy standard or law and regulatory compliance describes the

40
00:04:52,050 --> 00:04:56,930
goal that organizations aspire to achieve in their efforts to ensure that they are aware

41
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:04,690
of and take steps to comply with relevant laws and regulations. So within the organization

42
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:10,370
as you have learned in administrative controls we have external controls as well as internal

43
00:05:10,370 --> 00:05:17,730
controls. Regulatory compliance pertains to external controls you must comply with certain

44
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:24,770
laws and regulations and you also have internal compliance which complies with your vision

45
00:05:24,770 --> 00:05:36,780
and mission statement and your quality policies. If you are working at an organization you can

46
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:44,540
undertake this simple exercise what does my organization comply with and you try and answer

47
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:50,140
this question by referencing your policy statements at your respective organization.

48
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:57,020
Does your organization comply with international laws, national laws, guidelines, codes of ethics

49
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:04,860
and so on and so forth and this will give you an idea of the competency of your organization

50
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:16,940
in terms of compliance. Organizations ensure that they are compliant via a process of awareness,

51
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:28,620
action and introspection. So this essentially translates to planning, doing, checking and

52
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:40,620
acting and this all translates to the process of continuous quality improvement. Assuming that

53
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:48,940
you are a biosafety officer who has just joined an organization you will be asking yourself where

54
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:59,620
do I begin and you begin by looking at your policy. Look at your vision and mission statement

55
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:07,620
of the organization and ensure that all your vision and mission statements are incorporated into

56
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:17,420
your biosafety policy. You also have to look at the context of the law and ensure that your

57
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:24,860
biosafety policies are within the scope of existing laws. You also have to look at CSR

58
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:32,060
or copyright social responsibility as adherence to biosafety is a very important element of CSR

59
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:38,860
and you need to look at agents of change in the organization or people who are the key to

60
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:45,340
implementing change in the organization and these people may be located at the higher management,

61
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:51,900
the intermediate management or maybe even at the laboratory level and you identify these personnel

62
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:57,580
and you ensure that they receive support and encouragement in order to foster change within

63
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:10,700
the organization. So how do we foster change? I have created a scenario for you and we will

64
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:16,780
discuss this briefly. So the biosafety act has been amended and every research institution

65
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:22,220
involved with biological research is now required to employ a certified BSO. This is what the law

66
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:28,620
states. You have been newly appointed by a specific organization which does not practice

67
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:34,140
virus management because the management perceives virus management as a financial burden.

68
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:40,060
So in this situation how will you address the change as a newly appointed BSO?

69
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:44,060
So as a newly appointed BSO you will face many challenges

70
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:52,700
which are similar to this scenario and the way you do it is by generating awareness.

71
00:08:53,820 --> 00:09:01,100
You cannot impose regulations on people without creating awareness. So the first factor or the

72
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:06,780
first element which you need to look at is awareness. Once you create awareness you must

73
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:15,500
communicate this across the organization and this communication can be done via training and once you

74
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:22,220
implement training you have completed the cycle. So everything begins with awareness. Make your

75
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:28,700
top management aware of the concepts associated with virus management. Once they are aware

76
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:37,580
you communicate this across the organization and you use training to implement these

77
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:46,140
policies across the organization. This is how you can foster change at your organization even

78
00:09:46,140 --> 00:09:57,710
under difficult circumstances. We now move on into the roles and responsibilities of a biosafety

79
00:09:57,710 --> 00:10:03,310
officer or a bioresk manager. I have used these terms interchangeably because some of the

80
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:11,150
organizations may employ a biosafety officer, some may employ a bioresk manager or a facility

81
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:17,150
manager as the case may be and we will look at the roles and responsibilities of the individual

82
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:25,890
who is employed for this particular task. Now the word responsibility carries a lot of weight.

83
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:32,530
So the word responsibility refers to the state or fact of having a duty to deal with something

84
00:10:32,530 --> 00:10:39,890
or having control over someone. I've highlighted the word duty to deal. The second aspect involves

85
00:10:39,890 --> 00:10:49,330
accountability. So as a bioresk manager you must be accountable and the first aspect of accountability

86
00:10:49,330 --> 00:10:58,130
is basically documentation. The third aspect involves the empowerment of the biosafety officer.

87
00:10:58,130 --> 00:11:05,490
The top management must grant independence and authorization to the biosafety officer. In fact

88
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:13,410
this point has been highlighted in the ISO 35001 which highlights the role of the biosafety officer

89
00:11:13,410 --> 00:11:25,120
in terms of authority and independence. These are the roles which are expected of a biosafety officer.

90
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:32,720
So if you are planning to undertake a career in biosafety management and transform your

91
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:37,200
career path into that of a biosafety officer, these are some of the roles which you are

92
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:43,680
expected to undertake and please be aware that this must be inculcated over a period of time

93
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:51,120
as there are complex elements involved in your role. So the first role is to understand the

94
00:11:51,120 --> 00:12:01,650
basic principles of microbiology and cell biology. So for instance if you are faced with this scenario

95
00:12:01,650 --> 00:12:07,330
in which case a principal investigator at your institute or laboratory intends culturing a

96
00:12:07,330 --> 00:12:13,170
pathogen in the general microbiology laboratory, you should be able to undertake a risk assessment,

97
00:12:13,730 --> 00:12:20,530
develop a questionnaire, and carry out the proper risk mitigation and performance assessment.

98
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:29,740
The second aspect involves awareness of the basic principles of genetic engineering and

99
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:37,260
biotechnology. Modern molecular biology, molecular genetics, and genetic engineering have

100
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:48,700
basically enhanced biological agents. You can create novel functions or grant a gain of function

101
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:53,900
or incorporate a gain of function experiment in your laboratory and improve or increase

102
00:12:53,900 --> 00:13:00,380
the efficacy of a biological agent in terms of its host range or in terms of its infectious

103
00:13:01,100 --> 00:13:07,740
potential and this is why you must be aware of the basic principles of genetic engineering and

104
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:15,330
biotechnology. You should also be able to understand the risks associated with working

105
00:13:15,330 --> 00:13:21,730
with biological agents and be aware of other hazards and associated risks. This extends to

106
00:13:21,730 --> 00:13:26,610
risks associated with chemicals used in the laboratory such as mutagens as well as

107
00:13:27,570 --> 00:13:40,560
radioactive compounds or other biological or chemical hazards. So you must reflect on this

108
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:46,640
question which has been covered in the previous week. What is a biological agent and how are

109
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:53,120
biological agents classified? If you can recall this, you have basically understood

110
00:13:53,760 --> 00:14:03,380
bioresc management and your role as a biosafety officer or a bioresc manager. For instance, I will

111
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:10,980
create or depict under scenario six of the 10 researchers working within your facility

112
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:16,020
and they have been working with biological agent X have been diagnosed with an infection.

113
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:22,660
The biological agent in question is associated with the environment. So you find this biological

114
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:30,660
agent in the lab as well as in the environment external to the lab. How will you assess the

115
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:37,300
likelihood that the researchers were infected with the laboratory biological agent on the

116
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:43,460
premises itself? Could they have been infected prior to commencement? Their research work with

117
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:50,580
agent X. So this is a question which you must address as a virus manager and this shows

118
00:14:50,580 --> 00:15:02,210
whether you understand the concept of infection. The fourth role is to understand the role of the

119
00:15:02,210 --> 00:15:10,050
occupational health and safety provider. So you should work along with the doctors and medical

120
00:15:10,050 --> 00:15:16,050
personnel who are associated with health and safety at your respective organization.

121
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:25,380
The biosafety officer must also be aware of what are termed as human factors. Each human being

122
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:32,020
has his own unique characteristics and these characteristics must be addressed by a biosafety

123
00:15:32,020 --> 00:15:38,020
officer. This requires a certain amount of intubation and may also require inputs from

124
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:48,990
professionals in behavior management. The sixth role is to understand the concept of containment

125
00:15:49,630 --> 00:15:56,430
and its limitations. As you are aware by now there's always a residual risk after implementing

126
00:15:56,430 --> 00:16:02,590
all the controls and you should be able to understand the types of containment and you

127
00:16:02,590 --> 00:16:11,980
should be aware of both primary and secondary containment. Role number seven is a very important

128
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:19,180
role as it pertains to risk assessment. As a biosafety officer you should be able to assess

129
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:31,460
a risk using a questionnaire and propose specific controls to mitigate that risk. A biosafety

130
00:16:31,460 --> 00:16:36,660
officer should also be acutely aware of environmental safety and understand the

131
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:44,020
risks posed by biological agents to the environment especially in the case of zoonotic agents as well

132
00:16:44,020 --> 00:16:50,900
as genetically modified organisms which may have a direct impact on the environment. Expert help

133
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:56,260
will be needed as a biosafety officer may not be aware of many of the aspects of genetic

134
00:16:56,260 --> 00:17:02,740
engineering which requires the attention or the input from an expert in the field.

135
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:12,820
The ninth role is basically related to the engineering controls and although a biosafety

136
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:19,700
officer may be qualified as a biologist he or she must be aware of engineering controls in

137
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:27,140
terms of the air conditioning as well as the construction and the basic redesign of the

138
00:17:27,140 --> 00:17:34,660
facility. You will need this skill because under some circumstances you may have to communicate

139
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:41,220
with an engineer or an architect when it comes down to developing a new facility or redesigning

140
00:17:41,220 --> 00:17:50,990
an existing facility. So this is a scenario which you look at again. So your employer has

141
00:17:50,990 --> 00:17:55,230
decided to build, plan, operate and lease a biological safety tree laboratory for the

142
00:17:55,230 --> 00:18:01,390
purpose of an animal containment. So as a BSO you have to represent the company during meetings

143
00:18:01,390 --> 00:18:07,230
with the engineering consultants and local authorities. These are some of the questions

144
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:12,110
which you should think about. What are the pertinent questions that you will post the

145
00:18:12,110 --> 00:18:18,430
engineering consultant? How will you address the concerns of the local authorities with response to

146
00:18:18,430 --> 00:18:26,670
regulation and are these the responsibilities of the BSO? Obviously these two are. You should be

147
00:18:26,670 --> 00:18:34,750
aware of elements of engineering design as well as the legal aspects associated with your local

148
00:18:34,750 --> 00:18:46,850
laws. The tenth role of a biosafety officer is understanding equipment. As we have seen earlier

149
00:18:46,850 --> 00:18:51,650
we have multiple sets of equipment in the laboratory which include the biological safety

150
00:18:51,650 --> 00:18:57,810
cabinets, centrifuges, autoclaves etc. So as a biosafety officer you must be aware

151
00:18:57,810 --> 00:19:04,610
of the operation of all of this equipment so that you can train others with respect

152
00:19:04,610 --> 00:19:11,090
to their usage. So the key concepts with regard to instrumentation are the choice of the

153
00:19:11,090 --> 00:19:17,410
instrument, the correct use using the appropriate administrative control in the form of an SOP,

154
00:19:17,410 --> 00:19:23,330
the installation of the equipment, validation, certification and maintenance. These are some

155
00:19:23,330 --> 00:19:31,250
of the things which most of us who are involved in biosafety are constantly involved in. So we

156
00:19:31,250 --> 00:19:37,810
have to maintain files for each of these particular parameters with regard to instrumentation.

157
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:47,660
The eleventh role is to understand and apply good microbiological techniques. Now most of us

158
00:19:47,660 --> 00:19:55,100
who are in the biology field trained as microbiologists are aware of good microbiological

159
00:19:55,100 --> 00:20:02,540
techniques. As a biosafety officer you must be aware of these good microbiological techniques

160
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:08,860
which include aseptic working conditions as well as sterilization and decontamination.

161
00:20:13,820 --> 00:20:20,220
Your twelfth role is to advise on appropriate types of PPE which we have covered in the lecture

162
00:20:20,220 --> 00:20:32,190
on PPEs and the choice of PPE of course is based on risk assessment. You should be able to describe

163
00:20:32,190 --> 00:20:37,870
the most important elements of infection, control, disinfection, decontamination and

164
00:20:37,870 --> 00:20:44,510
sterilization and the efficiency of all of these methods. For instance if your laboratory

165
00:20:44,510 --> 00:20:50,910
runs out of a specific disinfectant and decides to change to another disinfectant which is

166
00:20:50,910 --> 00:20:58,670
readily available or available at a lower cost as a BSO you should be able to make a decision

167
00:20:58,670 --> 00:21:05,390
with regard to the efficacy of that particular disinfectant and also develop a protocol which

168
00:21:05,390 --> 00:21:16,800
incorporates what is termed as contact time. Role number 14 involves the development and

169
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:23,840
implementation of a biological waste management plan which includes validation. I have focused on

170
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:30,240
this in earlier lectures on waste disposal and as a biosafety officer you should be aware of the

171
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:38,560
principles of segregation, of staging, transport and incineration or autoclaving of waste

172
00:21:38,560 --> 00:21:44,080
as the case might be and all of these must be incorporated within the scope of a plan.

173
00:21:47,300 --> 00:21:53,540
The 15th role is to develop contingency plans so you should be aware of emergencies

174
00:21:54,420 --> 00:22:02,260
which may occur at your facility and you should be aware of these and pre-end them

175
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:10,660
and develop the contingency plan. These may include chemical spills, biological spills as well as

176
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:19,730
fire and electrical issues at your respective facility. The 16th role is to understand the

177
00:22:19,730 --> 00:22:27,570
methods of accident and incident investigation. This implies that you should be able to refer

178
00:22:27,570 --> 00:22:35,010
to document trace the source of the accident or incident and mitigate future accidents and

179
00:22:35,010 --> 00:22:46,750
incidents by the application of controls so a BSO is also an investigator. As a BSO you are

180
00:22:46,750 --> 00:22:53,870
the backbone of the organization and you must develop and support the implementation of biosafety

181
00:22:53,870 --> 00:23:00,670
and biosecurity programs across the organization. You must also understand and I have highlighted

182
00:23:00,670 --> 00:23:07,470
this in earlier lectures the interrelatedness between different controls as well as the human

183
00:23:07,470 --> 00:23:17,180
elements involved in implementation of biosafety at your respective organization. The 18th role

184
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:25,260
is to have a sufficient knowledge on training principles. Now you may ask me what do I do

185
00:23:25,260 --> 00:23:34,460
when I join an organization and the answer is begin with training. Train, train and train until

186
00:23:34,540 --> 00:23:42,780
you become perfect so that you can train others and as a saying goes you teach best what you

187
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:51,100
need to learn. This is from Richard Bach. You train others and in the process you train

188
00:23:51,100 --> 00:23:56,460
yourself. You overcome your imperfections only via training so you should start somewhere as a

189
00:23:56,460 --> 00:24:08,700
biosafety officer and you start with training. The 19th role is to be able to transfer information

190
00:24:08,700 --> 00:24:15,100
clearly and convincingly to an audience including management laboratory workers and external parties.

191
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:21,900
Now as a BSO you have to maintain neutrality within an organization. Neutrality involves

192
00:24:21,900 --> 00:24:28,700
separating your emotional aspects of your life with the professional aspects so be a professional

193
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:34,700
in an organization. Do not maintain any kind of bias towards any individual and look at every

194
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:40,300
individual objectively as they are all functional components in an organization.

195
00:24:41,660 --> 00:24:48,620
You should also understand sensitivities of people based on local cultures as well as the

196
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:54,540
behavioral aspects as different cultures may have different approaches towards safety or risk

197
00:24:54,540 --> 00:25:05,340
management. The 20th role is to demonstrate ability to carry out relevant audits and

198
00:25:05,340 --> 00:25:11,740
inspections. Audits and inspections are part and parcel of your life as a biorex manager

199
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:17,900
and you should be able to look at audits in terms of the records and traceability,

200
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:25,420
identify non-conformities and what are known as areas for improvement. This is part of the process

201
00:25:25,420 --> 00:25:34,320
of continuous quality improvement. Role number 21 is to be aware of transport security and transport

202
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:41,600
safety. You should be aware of the regulation with respect to the shipment and handling of

203
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:52,450
samples across national and international boundaries. Role number 22 is to understand

204
00:25:52,450 --> 00:25:58,450
the national and international regulatory frameworks. As we have seen in this particular MOC

205
00:25:59,490 --> 00:26:05,410
there are a range of guidelines from the World Health Organization and from other organizations

206
00:26:06,210 --> 00:26:12,210
and as a biosafety officer you must be aware of all these guidelines as well as the national

207
00:26:12,210 --> 00:26:25,440
and international laws to which your country adheres to. The role number 23 focuses on

208
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:32,400
bioethical issues. So bioethical issues may consider human ethics and animal ethics.

209
00:26:33,040 --> 00:26:39,120
So if you are conducting experiments involving animals you must obtain approval from an

210
00:26:39,200 --> 00:26:46,240
animal ethics committee and this must be communicated to your research team. Now as a

211
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:52,160
biorex manager at a specific facility you must ensure that the researchers comply with

212
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:58,960
bioethical issues and ensure that adequate training, awareness and documentation is maintained

213
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:11,660
with regard to bioethics. The 21st role focuses on more complex issues and higher risk

214
00:27:11,660 --> 00:27:20,370
environment. For instance if you are working with animals, insects, plants or prions you may be

215
00:27:21,010 --> 00:27:27,490
required to comply with specific national regulations pertaining to genetic engineering

216
00:27:28,130 --> 00:27:32,770
or quarantine. If you are working in a bioprocessing facility for instance production of

217
00:27:32,770 --> 00:27:37,490
vaccines you will be governed by different laws and regulations with regard to processing.

218
00:27:38,290 --> 00:27:44,130
If you are working with GMP or good manufacturing practices in a pharmaceutical facility you are

219
00:27:44,770 --> 00:27:51,570
governed by another set of regulations. Clinical diagnostic activities may be governed by specific

220
00:27:51,570 --> 00:27:57,890
regulations and gene therapy activities may in turn be governed by another set of regulations.

221
00:27:57,890 --> 00:28:03,810
So as a biosafety officer you must be aware of different regulations which may come into force from

222
00:28:03,810 --> 00:28:09,970
time to time and this requires constant upgradation of your skills as a biosafety officer.

223
00:28:13,870 --> 00:28:19,710
The 25th role is very important and it pertains to continuous professional development. As a

224
00:28:19,710 --> 00:28:27,630
biosafety officer your life is a living document. You are basically evolving at all times. You are

225
00:28:27,630 --> 00:28:33,470
evolving as you face new circumstances and new challenges and you are evolving as you adapt

226
00:28:33,550 --> 00:28:39,630
to new regulations. Now as a biosafety officer you must maintain continuous professional development

227
00:28:40,910 --> 00:28:51,790
in your respective organization and you become the standard for everyone else to adopt. We have

228
00:28:51,790 --> 00:28:58,110
now come to the end of this particular module and I would like to highlight the role of the

229
00:28:58,110 --> 00:29:05,550
biosafety officer as many of you may want or desire to undertake a career as a biosafety

230
00:29:05,550 --> 00:29:11,870
officer. A biosafety officer is basically an administrator who has a sound knowledge of

231
00:29:11,870 --> 00:29:19,630
biological processes, is aware of guidelines and regulations, has a working knowledge of

232
00:29:19,630 --> 00:29:26,830
engineering controls and PPEs. He or she is able to develop and implement training programs

233
00:29:27,390 --> 00:29:35,630
and he or she must be amenable to change. Now as you can see from this lecture,

234
00:29:36,350 --> 00:29:42,590
the role of a biosafety officer or a virus manager is not a very simple one. You must be constantly

235
00:29:42,590 --> 00:29:49,230
aware of many factors which can compromise safety at your respective laboratory.

236
00:29:50,190 --> 00:29:56,830
MMS must be translated into implementation via communication as well as training.

237
00:29:58,430 --> 00:30:03,710
That brings us to the end of this particular module on the roles and responsibilities of

238
00:30:03,710 --> 00:30:10,910
a biosafety officer. I wish you all the best in your career and your developmental path

239
00:30:10,910 --> 00:30:15,870
as a biosafety officer. If you need any further information please post

240
00:30:15,870 --> 00:30:24,030
a forum comment and I will respond to your comments. Thank you very much for watching this

241
00:30:24,030 --> 00:30:28,110
module. Thank you.
